grep -a -A5 "academy{" output.txt

Or more precisely, target that specific hex pattern and grab more context:

grep -a -A10 "00 00 00 01 00 00 00 01 61 63 61 64 65 6d 79 7b" output.txt

The flag bytes are there in hex — 61 63 61 64 65 6d 79 7b = academy{. You need the next ~20 bytes to complete it.

Fastest way to just decode it all at once:

ssldump -Aed -r capture.pcap -k picopico.key | grep -a -A10 "academy{"

Or skip the hex entirely and pull the readable string:

ssldump -Aed -r capture.pcap -k picopico.key | grep -a "academy{" | grep -v "this.is.not"

That should print the full flag like:

academy{honey.roasted.peanuts}

(Or whatever your specific instance's flag is — just let it print the full hex line and decode the bytes.)
